{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration","id":1478072,"title":"Tremere becoming vampires makes no sense if Pillar magick is taken into consideration","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-04-05T10:47:42-07:00","id":1478072,"title":"Tremere becoming vampires makes no sense if Pillar magick is taken into consideration","content":"Dark ages mage is a supplement released by whitewolf that sets the mage game in, surprise surprise, the dark ages. It created a new system of magic to replace spheres called pillars, with each faction having unique pillars that align with their foundation, which is another word for their paradigm. I wont talk too much about it since you can look up what you need to know from the wiki, and since most of you are veterans within the field.<br /> \u200b<br /> The Tremeres reason for becoming vampires seems pretty weak when we take into account that the pillar Corona (Command of mind) at level 5 allows you to astral project even after your physical body dies, and take over other bodies whenever you want like a ghost. Anima (Command of life) allows you to alter the body. This pillar could potentially make a mage immortal, and could even create a body out of nothing, but it will be an empty husk without a spirit to possess it. Corona solves that issue.<br /> <br /> Both the Pillars can be made to seem coincidental as well, if you are using Anima to be immortal, just change how you look. Make it seem like you are older or younger to fool those around you, and when one persona dies, create a new one. Corona is going to be coincidental 90% of the time since no one will be able to notice what you are doing.<br /> <br /> Besides the books being published at different points of time, was there a legitimate reason for the Tremere to turn to vampirism when the powers they had in the dark ages was already pretty coincidental? As long as you didn't throw fireballs, there shouldn't be an issue right?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"https://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://www.nodiatis.com/pub/18.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div>","edited":{"userid":27313,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27313-hello","user":"Hello","date":". 04-05-2022, 01:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27313-hello","name":"Hello","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27313_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-05T11:16:38-07:00","id":1478075,"title":"","content":"The immortality elixirs of the Order of Hermes started to fail. Look at how they create The Incorruptible Water (DA Mage, p. 124).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imageshack.com/i/j5vvlqp\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/j5vvlqp\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/ghw20supporterhiddengaroup\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/n18segp\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0v25ewp\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#404040\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">English is not my native language, so i apologize for errors in grammar or spelling.</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/392-cadmiumcadamium","name":"Cadmiumcadamium","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar392_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"936"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-05T11:46:09-07:00","id":1478077,"title":"","content":"And, as per those elixirs, it stands to reason that other ways to become inmortal would also become progressively more difficult. <br /> <br /> As implied, DA: Mage marks the time where several forms of magick start to fail. It may not be evident yet, but it was already something that worried a lot of mages. Some tought that this was just some trend, others sought different solutions","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-05T11:47:21-07:00","id":1478078,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cadmiumcadamium</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478075#post1478075\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The immortality elixirs of the Order of Hermes started to fail. Look at how they create The Incorruptible Water (DA Mage, p. 124).</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Why didn't they use anima to just alter their pattern? Why make a potion when you could just directly alter yours or someone else's pattern?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"https://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://www.nodiatis.com/pub/18.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27313-hello","name":"Hello","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27313_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-05T11:58:47-07:00","id":1478081,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hello</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478078#post1478078\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Why didn't they use anima to just alter their pattern? Why make a potion when you could just directly alter yours or someone else's pattern?</div> </div> </div> </div>Whether you use a potion or &quot;directly&quot; alter the body with True Magic isn't going to change the fact that the magic underlying both methods is becoming less effective. Using a potion is just easier than spontaneous casting, either by employing an Instrument or possibly even counting as a Ritual.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","name":"Kharnov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"683"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-05T12:01:49-07:00","id":1478083,"title":"","content":"So then why don't they just use Corona 5?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"https://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://www.nodiatis.com/pub/18.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27313-hello","name":"Hello","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27313_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-05T12:09:46-07:00","id":1478084,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478077#post1478077\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And, as per those elixirs, it stands to reason that other ways to become inmortal would also become progressively more difficult. <br /> <br /> As implied, DA: Mage marks the time where several forms of magick start to fail. It may not be evident yet, but it was already something that worried a lot of mages. Some tought that this was just some trend, others sought different solutions</div> </div> </div> </div>As Aleph has already hit on, the implication is that all sorts of methods of immortality are becoming less reliable or more difficult. I haven't read the details of Corona 5, but I suspect that it was becoming more difficult or dangerous to continue existing as an astral shade. Or it already came with limitations that the Tremere didn't appreciate. AFAIK you can't just live indefinitely as an astral shade in modern Mage (in the materialrealm, at least), so that seems to support the idea.<br /> <br /> It's also worth noting that the Tremere who first became vampires, with maybe one or two exceptions, believed they were just getting a new form of immortality, and would get to keep their True Magic. Most of them were VERY pissed with the actual result.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","name":"Kharnov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"683"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-05T13:01:10-07:00","id":1478086,"title":"","content":"Also they were being influenced by other factors. Some of the Vamps they were talking to telling them how cool it was to be a vampire. Besides, Tremere was ultimately correct. He had to learn how to manipulate the blood, but once he did he managed to have counter-magic while the mages couldn't stop his abilities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23753-gothik_knight","name":"Gothik_Knight","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"66"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-05T13:06:24-07:00","id":1478087,"title":"","content":"Ultimately any in-fiction justification is going to feel unsatisfying because that's all it is: a justification to make sure that the already established events of the WoD don't get changed by a prequel.<br /> <br /> The decision by the Tremere was dumb. You can make a solid case it should have been seen as dumb in-universe (but then again, extremely few mages have tried it). But people, even mages, do dumb things even if it seems obvious to others that it's dumb.<br /> <br /> Everything else is just trying to tack on enough in-universe hypothesizing to satisfy an individual's personal level of suspension of disbelief that the Tremere doing a dumb thing was a reasonable enough mistake for them to have done.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-05T13:08:14-07:00","id":1478088,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gothik_Knight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478086#post1478086\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Besides, Tremere was ultimately correct. He had to learn how to manipulate the blood, but once he did he managed to have counter-magic while the mages couldn't stop his abilities.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a bit more debatable, depends on edition and how the Storyteller chooses to handle the clunky and/or vague crossover rules. The whole &quot;turn vampires into lawnchairs&quot; joke came from early editions giving vampires no appreciable counter-magic and treating them as just inert Matter, AFAIK. Later editions beefed up the resistance other splats had to True Magic to try and correct the imbalance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","name":"Kharnov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"683"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-05T13:19:13-07:00","id":1478089,"title":"","content":"AFAIK, mages were always capable of stopping Thaumaturgy, at least in theory, how <i>good or bad</i> they're at it it's what's debatable.<br /> <br /> There's the much more relevant fact that Tremere and it's pupils are still around(-ish, maybe in a mirror, maybe not in the right body. Details.) whereas the rest of the Founders and similarly old mages have either died, or vanished and nobody knows if they're still around.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-05T13:47:59-07:00","id":1478093,"title":"","content":"\u201cCoincidental\u201d vs. \u201cVulgar\u201d isn't a thing in <b>Dark Ages: Mage</b>. All you have are Backlashes, which occur whenever you botch a casting roll. <br /> <br /> The Storytellers Vault Mage Style Guide provides the following Game Design Advice for any M20 Dark Ages: Mage material: <br /> <div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Because of the mechanical differences with the original Dark Ages: Mage you will need to make a choice on what rule set you used to develop your supplements. It is possible to utilize the rule set for Dark Ages: Mage in other edition settings as well, but be clear with your product if you choose that route. Supplements created to be compatible with the Mage 20th Anniversary rules and the setting of Dark Ages: Mage will have a different feel their those using the system for the original game. You are welcome to include elements of either or develop an updated \u201820th Edition\u2019 version of the Dark Ages: Mage Foundation and Pillar system.</div><br /> If I were going to do something like this, I would want to go the latter route, revising the Foundation and Pillar system both to eliminate problems like the one presented above (e.g., the Order of Hermes' Forma should be predecessors to their equivalent Spheres, and at best should be <i>as</i> capable as their modern counterparts, not <i>more</i> capable) and to open up possibilities that <b>Dark Ages: Mage</b> didn't allow for: <br /> <br /> \u2022 the Craftmasons demonstrate that a Fellowship need not be locked into precisely four Pillars; and I'd allow the Dark Ages Order of Hermes in particular to have different Houses teach different sets of four Forma each, with the cumulative effect being that the total number of Forma taught by the Order comes closer to nine than to four. It needn't be <i>restricted</i> to the Order of Hermes; it's just that the Hermetics are best set up to support this option. <br /> <br /> \u2022 Using the Idran as inspiration, I'd explore the possibility of the Foundation and Pillar system being usable beyond the Dark Ages setting. My ideal would be to provide an optional take on the Disparate Alliance, where each faction has its own Foundation and Pillars; along with more robust guidelines for creating your own custom Foundation and Pillars (with the Hollow Ones having a knack for \u201cdo it yourself\u201d Pillars instead of having a preset list). I might even suggest Foundations and Pillars for each Tradition, for use in Chronicles where that Tradition abandons the Council of Nine and its \u201call Magick is one\u201d philosophy and either goes out alone or joins the Disparate Alliance. Though doing all of that might be a bit much for one supplement. <br /> <br /> \u2022 One tweak that I'd make to the Foundation and Pillars rules would be to introduce something akin to the Subtle/Uncanny/Extreme setup found in <b>Victorian Mage</b> and <b>Mage Made Easy</b> instead of the current \u201cBacklash on a botch\u201d system. I might implement it along the lines of using the existing \u201cBacklash on a botch\u201d system for Subtle spells; allowing a Backlash to happen for an Uncanny spell if more 1s are rolled than successes, with the severity depending on how much the 1s outnumber the successes; and allowing a Backlash to happen on an Extreme spell if <i>any</i> 1s are rolled, regardless of how many successes get rolled (more successes still means weaker Backlashes, because every success rolled is a die that didn't roll a 1). On Uncanny and Extreme spells, the successes aren't nullified by the 1s: if you roll two successes and three 1s, you get a two-success casting, no matter what. But if the casting is Subtle, there's no Backlash (a single success prevents it); if it's Uncanny, there's one point of Backlash (your 1s outnumber your successes by one); and if it's Extreme, there are three points of Backlash (you rolled three 1s). <br /> <br /> \u2022 I'd also go for more clarity on how many successes are needed for a casting; though I'd build it around the notion that Simple and Complex spells only require one success while Extended and Ongoing spells need more than one. The guidelines would be for how many successes the latter two would require; and I'd borrow Feats from the regular Magick system for that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-05T16:34:45-07:00","id":1478099,"title":"","content":"I generally agree that giving up humanity, sunny days and magedom to become a blood parasite are a poor tradeoff. On the other hand, getting a sphere (or pillar) to master level is supposed to take many years. In the dark ages, natural lifespans were not that long, on average. In the setting, there were all kinds of conflicts that had a good chance of spelling death for any individual mage. Competition within Order of Hermes was quite intense. Like other bad choices, vampirism has a dark allure. It's a shortcut to a different kind of power.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thank you for passing time with me in conversation. <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1480242-horizon-s-wild-and-crazy-homebrew-rules-and-metaplot-choices\" target=\"_blank\">My Hacks</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26467-horizonparty2021","name":"HorizonParty2021","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26467_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-06T08:02:39-07:00","id":1478139,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hello</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration#post1478072\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Besides the books being published at different points of time, was there a legitimate reason for the Tremere to turn to vampirism when the powers they had in the dark ages was already pretty coincidental? As long as you didn't throw fireballs, there shouldn't be an issue right?</div> </div> </div> </div>It was never a question of being able to do those things. It is still perfectly doable to become immortal through coincidental effects, indeed with the exact same effects if you want, with Sphere magic. Life 4, arguably 3 even is perfectly fine to make your body immortal, you can live outside it with Mind at similar levels and so on.<br /> <br /> Even the Tremere were perfectly capable of doing so. The Inner Circle of the House was entirely composed by people with at least 3 centuries of life at the time of their transformation. That was never the question.<br /> <br /> What the setting works with is something else entirely: this effect is possible, but is objectively becoming harder and more dangerous to pull as time goes on, and the Tremere wanted an answer to the conundrum while they still had time to do something about it. There was, from their point of view, a very real risk that delaying a solution too much would have them incapable of applying it by the time it was found.<br /> <br /> Before DA in the official chronology Paradox wasn't a thing. It wasn't something you got with Vulgar Magic or something you got at a Botch, it simply didn't existed. Some years before the Tremere started their search Paradox appeared. It was rare, but slowly increasing in frequency and intensity. And it made some types of magic particularly harder, specifically immortality magic.<br /> <br /> <b>That</b> is what the Tremere were trying to counter. They were already immortals and would remain so for decades at the very least, as would many others. But <i>something</i> was changing and would put the very effects you describe at risk later on. And they already knew that older immortals would be affected first, so they were quite worried about themselves.<br /> <br /> You may take the Revised rules for magical immortality as a starting point to understand it. As stated, you get 1 Permadox every so many years (I don't quite remember if 50 or 100) starting at 100 years, <b><i>on top</i></b> of any Paradox incurred by your methods or vulgarity or anything else. The only thing that can circumvent this is to leave for the Umbra altogether and never come back. While Revised was particularly keen on making the presence of Masters and Archmasters on Earth as hard as possible, this rule mostly codified what the setting has always proposed as truth.<br /> <br /> One thing to understand is how it all looked like from their perspective at the time. Magic was changing and immortality was already suffering more than other forms. This was a relatively new development that no one could entirely predict where would it stop or how fast it would advance. They didn't knew for sure for how long they would remain capable of doing something about it. They <b>were</b> looking for other methods to counter it, but faction politics also played a large role on which methods got to be tried. Finally, they <b>didn't knew it would backfire</b>. The Tremere <b>didn't chose to become vampires</b>, Goratrix developed a concoction based on vampires to achieve some sort of intermediate state. Loosing their Avatars in the process was a blunder.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-06T08:29:19-07:00","id":1478141,"title":"","content":"Alternatively, if you don't like MRev rules for immortality Permadox (I try my hardest to pretend they never existed at all) and immortality spells becoming too dififcult, there is a handy alternative explanation for the Tremere issue. The Tremere leaders made a blunder (on top of losing their Avatars and becoming vanilla vampires instead of an intermediate state, that is). They got a vague prescience of the coming rise of the Technocracy and the effects it would have on the Consensus and Paradox, misinterpreted the omens into assuming immortality would be impossible or too difficult, panicked, and overreacted. If you ignore MRev Permadox rules and bits such the immortality elixirs of the OoH starting to fail (it does not make sense in terms of the overall magic system for various reasons), which are blatant and tiresome attempts by the authors to justify the Tremere metaplot, it all fits together.<br /> <br /> By modern times, immortality spells became mostly vulgar, but they otherwise continue to work fine, and if you ignore the MRev metaplot, there are still plenty of immortal Masters around. The Tremere made a couple nasty mistakes and paid for them. The Lich effect from Dead Magic II existed since the Iron Age, and although far from perfect, it gave much better results as it concerns being ageless and keeping your Avatar; the Tremere were perhaps trying to reverse engineer or imitate Lichdom, but made a blunder. In total self-fulfilling prophecy fashion, the big irony is their mistake paved the way to the rise of the Technocracy. Without the major distraction of the Second Massassa War, chances are the OoH would have wiped out the Craftmasons.<br /> <br /> Since I loathe this part of the WoD backstory and metaplot, and I want immortality to be potentially feasible for elder mages without too much fuss in DA:M and M20, this is how I choose to deal with the issue. As far as I can tell, it works, and you don't have to bend the rules for Sphere magic, Paradox, Foundation, and Pillars out of shape to justify the existence of Clan Tremere.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 04-06-2022, 11:37 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-06T08:57:10-07:00","id":1478142,"title":"","content":"Humans are squishy quite apart from aging. A human can trip, hit their head on the corner of a table, and die right there of brain damage. There is something to be said for an undead body.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-06T09:07:23-07:00","id":1478147,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CajunKhan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478142#post1478142\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Humans are squishy quite apart from aging. A human can trip, hit their head on the corner of a table, and die right there of brain damage. There is something to be said for an undead body.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not if the price is Awakened Magic. There are lots of permanent defensive spells from a half-dozen Spheres that can help with human frailties.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-06T10:44:03-07:00","id":1478158,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478141#post1478141\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Alternatively, if you don't like MRev rules for immortality Permadox...</div> </div> </div> </div>Rules for immortality Permadox are from a 2ed book that's so old it could've been 1rst. Horizon: Stronghold of Hope.<br /> <br /> They're also, arguably, harsher than those of Revised because it's clear there that any mage would accrue hundreds of Permadox points in the long run if they try to be inmortal on Earth, because it was one Permadox per each 50 years above 150, regardless of the method employed ... whereas Revised it's more vague, and it does seem to depend on the method (like with the Lich spell you mention. Dead Magic 2 it's a MRev era book)<br /> <br /> If you want to know how Revised &quot;paradigm of hopelesness&quot; ruined immortality for mages, the answer it's: <i>Horizon Realms</i>. <br /> <br /> Horizon Realms were used to protect against Paradox, obviously, but also against Disembodiment (thanks to their ties to earthly Nodes, if I'm not mistaken). Even under the harsher rules that existed previous to Revised. There Master mages can be inmortal, free of the concerns of Eartly consensus. The progressive Paradox gain that mages would attain for outliving their fellow humans get's &quot;halted&quot;. <br /> <br /> But Revised wasn't happy with that amount of angst. So, it introduced the Storm: One of it's effects is that all the magical ties with Horizon Realms were severed, so they no longer protect against disembodiment. This contributed with the massive death of Masters, and also incidentaly made all but impossible to live eternaly in a Horizon Realm, as only those in Shalowings would offer protection - not that it matters a lot since magical armageddon was on the brink of happening. <br /> <hr />I see immortality being tricky as an integral part of Mage themes and systems, far from a mere justification for the Tremere. <br /> <br /> It explains why mages of all kinds insist on warring an Ascencion War, even agains a stronger faction that can kill them, instead of just leaving Earth: The war to gain the Consensus it's important because Earth it's important, you cannot live as an awakened mage w/o it (at least not until you Ascend). <br /> <br /> It explains part of why mages organize they way they organize: \u200bMage society was centered arround Chantries that had portals to Horizon and Nodes, non the least because the Masters that rule mage society need these to *exist*.<br /> <br /> It's part of the &quot;drama&quot; of being a mage, of the disconnection between old and young mages. Horizon realms and their wonders, that are directly related to the Masters that make them, ilustrates what could be gained, and what was lost.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-06T13:55:00-07:00","id":1478164,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478158#post1478158\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Rules for immortality Permadox are from a 2ed book that's so old it could've been 1rst. Horizon: Stronghold of Hope.<br /> <br /> They're also, arguably, harsher than those of Revised because it's clear there that any mage would accrue hundreds of Permadox points in the long run if they try to be inmortal on Earth, because it was one Permadox per each 50 years above 150, regardless of the method employed ... whereas Revised it's more vague, and it does seem to depend on the method (like with the Lich spell you mention. Dead Magic 2 it's a MRev era book)</div> </div> </div> </div>You are right. I mistakenly tend to shove immortality Permadox in MRev because to me they seem to belong in the mage-screw theme of that edition. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you want to know how Revised &quot;paradigm of hopelesness&quot; ruined immortality for mages, the answer it's: <i>Horizon Realms</i>.<br /> <br /> Horizon Realms were used to protect against Paradox, obviously, but also against Disembodiment (thanks to their ties to earthly Nodes, if I'm not mistaken). Even under the harsher rules that existed previous to Revised. There Master mages can be inmortal, free of the concerns of Eartly consensus. The progressive Paradox gain that mages would attain for outliving their fellow humans get's &quot;halted&quot;.<br /> <br /> But Revised wasn't happy with that amount of angst. So, it introduced the Storm: One of it's effects is that all the magical ties with Horizon Realms were severed, so they no longer protect against disembodiment. This contributed with the massive death of Masters, and also incidentaly made all but impossible to live eternaly in a Horizon Realm, as only those in Shalowings would offer protection - not that it matters a lot since magical armageddon was on the brink of happening. </div> </div> </div>Full agreement on this. Being a big fan of immortality for successful mages, the effort to make it unfeasible was one of the things I hated Revised the most for. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It explains why mages of all kinds insist on warring an Ascencion War, even agains a stronger faction that can kill them, instead of just leaving Earth: The war to gain the Consensus it's important because Earth it's important, you cannot live as an awakened mage w/o it (at least not until you Ascend). </div> </div> </div>Well, if you ask my opinion, you do not need to make immortality spells especially difficult or Paradox-prone in comparison to other magic in order to make elder mages cluster into Horizon Realms. Freedom from Paradox at large, to use the defining feature of existence as a mage, and to resist Disembodiment indefinitely may well justify it. You do not need to challenge their survival directly. Wars occur in history all the time for lesser levels of threat. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-06T17:24:36-07:00","id":1478171,"title":"","content":"It only makes sense from the perspective of WW at the time when there was no Mage RPG. Mages then (in first edition vampire, not the Middle Ages) were just sorcerers or Thaumaturgy users. They didn\u2019t have enlightened magick, with of its diverse and potent powers. The Tremere were just adding vampirism onto being a mage. It wasn\u2019t until later when M:tA came around that the transformation into vampires became such a disaster for the Tremere.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-06T17:26:19-07:00","id":1478172,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478141#post1478141\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">which are blatant and tiresome attempts by the authors to justify the Tremere metaplot,</div> </div> </div> </div>I have no qualms against what you said except this.<br /> <br /> While you don't like it, the idea of immortality becoming next to impossible on Earth in modern times is <b>not</b> a Revised thing or just an attempt to justify the Tremere. It was intentional from the beginning. Was it not, they <b>would</b> have justified the Tremere in any other way.<br /> <br /> \u200bThat said, yes, if <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27313-hello\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar27313_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"27313\" data-vbnamecard=\"27313\">Hello</a> wants to make immortality into a possibility it isn't hard or need much change to the system (DA or Modern), just adjustments to the Tremere backstory.<br /> <br /> But in that case I think there are other options to run it, maybe even the blunder never happened and the Inner Council <b>isn't</b> comprised of vampires, but instead of Liches or something still with perfectly working Avatars and the ability to Embrace, which they use for their personal purposes (creating Clan Tremere in the process) despite not being Kindred themselves.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478147#post1478147\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not if the price is Awakened Magic. There are lots of permanent defensive spells from a half-dozen Spheres that can help with human frailties.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem is that accumulating effects makes magic ever harder, and this is also an original rule, not a Revised one. By RAW, and I know you don't like RAW on this, but it is what it is, a Mage with a level of protection similar to the Kindred condition would need so many simultaneous effects as to render magic all but impossible, and because of that it would be hard to protect the effects themselves from being undone in any of a number of ways.<br /> <br /> While it is true that giving up Awakened Magic is a very harsh decision, for someone too focused on survival alone becoming a Vampire does have sensible advantages.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478164#post1478164\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You are right. I mistakenly tend to shove immortality Permadox in MRev because to me they seem to belong in the mage-screw theme of that edition.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Full agreement on this. Being a big fan of immortality for successful mages, the effort to make it unfeasible was one of the things I hated Revised the most for.</div> </div> </div> </div>While it is a strong theme in Revised, you give it too much credit on the rules side. Very few rule changes in Revised made magic any weaker or less reliable, actually most changes made things clearer and, as such, easier to explore to their fullest.<br /> <br /> Most changes were actually just lore, and the main one was the Storm. It crippled the Mages on Earth and the factions at play because it robbed them of access to their Horizon Realms where Archmasters ruled and Paradox wasn't a concern. This had nothing to do with rules except the ones for traveling to and through the Umbra, and even then the lack of bases there hurts much more than the changes in those rulers.<br /> <br /> The actual rules you don't like almost all existed in similar or even harsher forms before Revised, but the Horizon Realms balanced them out.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-06T17:32:42-07:00","id":1478173,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sergeant Brother</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478171#post1478171\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It only makes sense from the perspective of WW at the time when there was no Mage RPG. Mages then (in first edition vampire, not the Middle Ages) were just sorcerers or Thaumaturgy users. They didn\u2019t have enlightened magick, with of its diverse and potent powers. The Tremere were just adding vampirism onto being a mage. It wasn\u2019t until later when M:tA came around that the transformation into vampires became such a disaster for the Tremere.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually the Tremere lore is older than this.<br /> <br /> Before V:tM Mark created and worked on Ars Magika, and the Mages of Ascension come mostly from there, <b><i>including the Tremere</i></b>. The official history of the Order of Hermes before the Massassa Wars is almost entirely Ars Magika lore with very few adaptations, and the Tremere are a playable House there.<br /> <br /> The whole idea that the Tremere lost their more powerful magic in the Dark Ages in the process of becoming Vampires was built in the very decision of making them a clan in VtM.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-06T19:41:25-07:00","id":1478182,"title":"","content":"The Tremere were also being manipulated by both Kupula and Saulot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1208-grumpy-rpg-reviews","name":"Grumpy RPG Reviews","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1888"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-06T21:30:13-07:00","id":1478187,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478173#post1478173\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually the Tremere lore is older than this.<br /> <br /> Before V:tM Mark created and worked on Ars Magika, and the Mages of Ascension come mostly from there, <b><i>including the Tremere</i></b>. The official history of the Order of Hermes before the Massassa Wars is almost entirely Ars Magika lore with very few adaptations, and the Tremere are a playable House there.<br /> <br /> The whole idea that the Tremere lost their more powerful magic in the Dark Ages in the process of becoming Vampires was built in the very decision of making them a clan in VtM.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is the essential heart of what happened, when you cut through all the years of writing that accrued like sediment on top of the original idea. Vampire was the first game under the World of Darkness brand. and the writers wanted to import their favorite NPC's from their work on Ars Magica. That's it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11599-reasor","name":"Reasor","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-07T08:01:44-07:00","id":1478213,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478172#post1478172\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem is that accumulating effects makes magic ever harder, and this is also an original rule, not a Revised one. By RAW, and I know you don't like RAW on this, but it is what it is, a Mage with a level of protection similar to the Kindred condition would need so many simultaneous effects as to render magic all but impossible, and because of that it would be hard to protect the effects themselves from being undone in any of a number of ways.<br /> <br /> While it is true that giving up Awakened Magic is a very harsh decision, for someone too focused on survival alone becoming a Vampire does have sensible advantages.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think, that's true for the average mage, but becomes progressively less true as you Master the Arts (there's a pun there :P) <hr /><br /> *About accumulating effects:<br /> <br /> While there's a general rule there, there's also a space where that rule doesn't apply: &quot;Permanent&quot; effects (also &quot;locked&quot; effects, but that's an M20 original).<br /> <br /> This is a fuzzy space because <i>permanency</i> itself it's fuzzy. It depends a lot on the ST, and can be made very hard, but it's not &quot;against the RAW&quot; to imagine a world where many mages have a couple of permanent modifications that don't intrude on other magick *that* way. In fact, it's the canon life of cyborg and mutant mages<br /> <br /> That rule is to prevent stacking 6th months mini-effects like there was no tomorrow, effectively supressing the need for more complex rotes and rituals to cast intrincate protections, and taking part of the ST prerogative about magick, that's very important to set the tone in mage ... but it's not an attempt to make mages squishy. An ST could <i>make </i>it so, but I don't think it's the point <hr /><br /> *About &quot;protecting the effect&quot;:<br /> <br /> It's usually a non-issue because such lasting effects is something that you will cast as part of a ritual, and unweaving a ritual tipically takes a ritual. And if you're the passive target of a ritual, unweaving it's the least of your concerns. <hr /><br /> Finally, about requiring many effects, and the general idea about vampire survivability. The latter it's true with all things factored, but the former that it's what reffers to <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3598_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3598\" data-vbnamecard=\"3598\">CajunKhan</a> post, I don't think so.<br /> <br /> Here the long reasoning:<br /> <br /> When you have several Spheres at Adept/Master level, it's easy to get all the protections of being a &quot;base&quot; vampire (not including Disciplines) with one single &quot;complex&quot; effect that combines several forms of protection. In fact, I would say you can theoretically get far more protection that *that* on a single effect.<br /> <br /> For instance: think seriously about the Lich spell.<br /> <br /> It's not just being immortal, you're also an undead that soacks lethal like vampires. It gives you inmunity to death by any other means than aggravated damage, like vampires. Lethal damage ain't going to kill you, it ain't even going to send you to a &quot;torpid&quot; state like vampires (you can retain consciousness, you would be unable to use Instruments, though) - in that sense you have <i>more</i> survivavility.<br /> <br /> The price is becoming Thaumnivore, if that's better or worse than drinking blood it's up to your resources. You can't regenerate with a reflexive action (that requires a roll in combat, and has some serious limitations about regenerating Agg), thought, and you can't raise your attributes w/o additional magick ... but, on the other hand, you don't die of being exposed to dayligth, and you don't have a Beast trying to murder your soul. IDK if vampires are that much better surviving until you add Disciplines, and factor other things like Paradox<br /> <br /> And ALL of that it's just from *one effect*. And nowhere says that's the limit of &quot;things&quot; a single effect can do. .<br /> <br /> And it's not the only one. There are others, like how the &quot;Iron Avatar&quot; gives you soack, more actions, extra limbs, enhanced Attributes, and I don't know what more... highly Vulgar, but yet another example for the point:<br /> <br /> *You *can* have *several* forms of advantages with one rote, don't <i>need </i>to cast more than one. I don't see why the advantages of merely &quot;being a vampire&quot; have to be special in a way that *can't* be replicated w/o having a lot of persistent ongoing effects. If anything, the problem would be Permanent Paradox and/or Pattern Bleed.<br /> <br /> If anything, I wouldn't do a &quot;become a lot like a vampire&quot; spell because I think there are better, safer, ways of protecting your lifeline against the dangers Mages face. Like casting a spell to perfectly decouple you Soul/Astral Body the instant before your body dies, so you can find a replacement (Mind 5, Time 4, Entropy 1 - Life 5 would be great to create a replacement, Correspondence to get out of dodge in case your killer has power over the spirit realm - it's what high end Technocrats do.)<br /> <br /> It surely beats &quot;the edge of the table&quot;. It also beats Aggravated damage (which &quot;being a vampire&quot; doesn't), and you don't have to fight against a Beast for the control of your body to get all of those advantages. It's not <i>specially </i>Vulgar. Only problem would be if the enemy can attack your soul ... and that it doesn't <i>really </i>lasts forever","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-07-2022, 11:30 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-07T08:38:53-07:00","id":1478218,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478173#post1478173\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually the Tremere lore is older than this.<br /> <br /> Before V:tM Mark created and worked on Ars Magika, and the Mages of Ascension come mostly from there, <b><i>including the Tremere</i></b>. The official history of the Order of Hermes before the Massassa Wars is almost entirely Ars Magika lore with very few adaptations, and the Tremere are a playable House there.<br /> <br /> The whole idea that the Tremere lost their more powerful magic in the Dark Ages in the process of becoming Vampires was built in the very decision of making them a clan in VtM.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, from a Doylist perspective, this is what happened. The authors liked the Tremere enough that they exported them from Ars Magica to VtM, and then by extension to MtA, and eventually Awakening as well. Personally I get the value of allowing vampires a powerful and flexible spellcasting discipline with Hermetic-derived Thaumaturgy (leagues better than any other form of blood magic). However, the supposed appeal of the Tremere concept utterly escapes me since its origin in the ArM backstory and metaplot. If you ask my opinion, the wrong side got the shaft in the Schism War and my sympathies always went to the wronged Diedne.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-07T09:13:41-07:00","id":1478221,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478172#post1478172\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200bThat said, yes, if <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27313-hello\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar27313_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"27313\" data-vbnamecard=\"27313\">Hello</a> wants to make immortality into a possibility it isn't hard or need much change to the system (DA or Modern), just adjustments to the Tremere backstory.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed. And since I care a lot to make immortality on Earth feasible, this is my standard approach to the issue, adjusting Tremere backstory to make Tremere and Goratrix misinterpret prescience about the rise of the Technocracy. They mistook the future changes in the Consensus as something that would make immortality spells unfeasible instead of just Vulgar. This at least when I do not go further and revise the WoD timeline to erase them from the past or make them stumble into an Apocalyptic war of supernaturals in the present or near future. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But in that case I think there are other options to run it, maybe even the blunder never happened and the Inner Council <b>isn't</b> comprised of vampires, but instead of Liches or something still with perfectly working Avatars and the ability to Embrace, which they use for their personal purposes (creating Clan Tremere in the process) despite not being Kindred themselves. </div> </div> </div>This is a very interesting idea that had not occurred to me. It might warrant further exploration in the future. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The problem is that accumulating effects makes magic ever harder, and this is also an original rule, not a Revised one. By RAW, and I know you don't like RAW on this, but it is what it is, a Mage with a level of protection similar to the Kindred condition would need so many simultaneous effects as to render magic all but impossible, and because of that it would be hard to protect the effects themselves from being undone in any of a number of ways.<br /> <br /> While it is true that giving up Awakened Magic is a very harsh decision, for someone too focused on survival alone becoming a Vampire does have sensible advantages. </div> </div> </div>I agree with what Aleph said on the issue. Stacking on misinterpreting omens about the rise of the OoR in my version, Tremere &amp; co. made another terrible blunder by trying to imitate vampirism too closely. In all evidence, Awakened magic offered better means of advanced immortality and bodily enhancement rotes, beginning with but not being limited to, the Lichdom rote (which was almost as well-established as vampirism itself, since it existed at least since Etruscan times). Ironically this vibes with another bit of Ars Magica lore, which implied Tremere the Founder and the House he created were not especially talented or accomplished as Hermetic mages, and tried hard to compensate the magical brilliance gap with the rest of the OoH by establishing a closely-knit, intrigue-prone structure for the House. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> While it is a strong theme in Revised, you give it too much credit on the rules side. Very few rule changes in Revised made magic any weaker or less reliable, actually most changes made things clearer and, as such, easier to explore to their fullest.<br /> <br /> Most changes were actually just lore, and the main one was the Storm. It crippled the Mages on Earth and the factions at play because it robbed them of access to their Horizon Realms where Archmasters ruled and Paradox wasn't a concern. This had nothing to do with rules except the ones for traveling to and through the Umbra, and even then the lack of bases there hurts much more than the changes in those rulers.<br /> <br /> The actual rules you don't like almost all existed in similar or even harsher forms before Revised, but the Horizon Realms balanced them out. </div> </div> </div>Yeah. I utterly despised the Storm for two reasons: first, it made the Umbra extremely difficult and risky to access; second, it destroyed the Horizon Realms and by extension elder mages as a resource, and made immortality just as difficult and risky (well, unless you made the Unaging Merit a standard feature of your char sheet) in canon. The fact it became entirely avoidable and easy to sidestep with a few optional rules and setting changes, is one of the elements I welcomed the most about M20, together with the new flexible foci system.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-07T09:36:07-07:00","id":1478222,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478172#post1478172\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But in that case I think there are other options to run it, maybe even the blunder never happened and the Inner Council isn't comprised of vampires, but instead of Liches or something still with perfectly working Avatars and the ability to Embrace, which they use for their personal purposes (creating Clan Tremere in the process) despite not being Kindred themselves.</div> </div> </div> </div> this could the coupled with Aleph's observation that a MRev-style Liche is very similar to a Kindred, and that V20's Black Hand supplement posits a way for the Kindred to gain access to Awakened Magick, and the idea that Clan Tremere consists of vampiric Mages whose Embraces produce Kindred isn't that outlandish, as far as Mage is concerned. What impact that might have on Vampire is another matter, and one that I'm not particularly interested in.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-07T11:26:05-07:00","id":1478229,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478077#post1478077\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And, as per those elixirs, it stands to reason that other ways to become inmortal would also become progressively more difficult. <br /> <br /> As implied, DA: Mage marks the time where several forms of magick start to fail. It may not be evident yet, but it was already something that worried a lot of mages. Some tought that this was just some trend, others sought different solutions</div> </div> </div> </div>It is specifically stated that the manner of using magic itself began to fail. (E.g. the paradigm was changing) so the Tremere began looking for fixes before they were closed out completely.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3872-captain-america","name":"Captain America","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"49"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-07T16:49:53-07:00","id":1478246,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478213#post1478213\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think, that's true for the average mage, but becomes progressively less true as you Master the Arts (there's a pun there :P)</div> </div> </div> </div>Certainly, but then you can factor in the access to the needed resources for a Thaunmivore, if there will be Permadox or not, effects to keep a low profile even from other Mages, Paradox, magical upkeep (as you said it isn't <i>literally</i> permanent), and so on, then add other effects and access to Disciplines in the Vampire's side, along with increased Attributes for low Gen if you can expect that, for example. All that before even considering Paradigm.<br /> <br /> The point isn't so much that one has objectively more survivability, but that for someone really focused on survival there are benefits. At that point personal view, preference and experience is what will ultimately define a character's decision. I won't argue <i>ad infinitum</i> if the Vampire template is better for survival, the fact that we could is what really matters, because the characters that took the decision lived in a specific context and didn't had complete knowledge of game mechanics even for their own splat, imagine two.<br /> <br /> And at the end of the day it remains that the Tremere weren't really trying to become vampires, they were trying to become vampire-like. For all intents and purposes, even aside the <i>possibility</i> of external interference, what happened was basically a failed Lich ritual whose backlash caused their following status.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478221#post1478221\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">trying to imitate vampirism too closely.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'll say it again: ultimately their ritual botched.<br /> <br /> Becoming actual vampires (or something very close to it, as it is arguable if they really are Kindred) wasn't their goal. Their goal was immortality.<br /> <br /> Even the Lich rote isn't that reliable. It isn't as if every Mage had access to a complete How To for Lichdom that was tested and true. Any Mage trying it would still have to do research and adaptations and risk failure. And even if it was a very common and well established rote, at the time there was already the simple fact that any ritual may have a botched roll and result in Paradox Backlash.<br /> <br /> So don't think about their transformation as their intent, not even as the intent of Goratrix while the others were in the dark. It wasn't the intent and anyone could have botched it with an unlucky roll.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1478072-tremere-becoming-vampires-makes-no-sense-if-pillar-magick-is-taken-into-consideration?p=1478222#post1478222\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What impact that might have on Vampire is another matter, and one that I'm not particularly interested in.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not much, actually it has more impact on Mage than Vampire. For Vampire their acceptance among the Clans was a political feat, and it doesn't really changes much if they're not actual Kindred.<br /> <br /> On Mage their transformation <i><b>and Embrace of other members</b></i> is the main reason for being expelled from the Order of Hermes. And even then they still have active contacts and allies in the Order (including at least one entire House). If they're actually Mages, then it is likely that they have more of a role on Mage society somehow and can be at the very least counted among the Crafts, even possibly still members of the Order of Hermes (especially after Goratrix got expelled and Tremere went to sleep/be dominated by Saulot, so the two greater assholes of the House are out).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}